ctually baseline sequential time 0.671s, best parallel 0.1764s speedup ~3.8x. That's close to 4 cores ideal. Maybe we can't do much better. But we need to propose something anyway.\n\nMaybe try using schedule(static) with chunk size = n/num_threads (default) but also use #pragma omp parallel for on both loops and combine them into one loop over i that does force then update? That would require updating positions after forces computed, which depends on all forces for that body only; we could compute force and immediately update position in same iteration since velocity is updated within body_force. Actually body_force updates p[i].vx += dt*fx etc. Then position update uses new velocities. So if we parallelize outer loop i and inside each iteration do both force computation (inner j) and then position update, that would be correct because for a given i, velocity is updated before position update in same thread. But careful: body_force currently updates p[i].vx after computing fx; then main loop does positions using updated velocities. If we combine into one parallel for over i where each iteration computes forces and then updates position, that's equivalent to sequential order? Sequential order: for iter, first compute all forces (updating velocities) for all bodies, then update positions for all bodies. If we do per-i force+position in same loop, the velocity of body i is updated before its position, but other bodies' velocities are not yet updated when computing forces for i? Wait, force computation for i reads p[j] positions and velocities? Actually it only reads positions (p[j].x,y,z) and uses them to compute dx etc. It does not read velocities. So force on i depends only on positions of all bodies at start of iteration. If we update position of body i immediately after computing its velocity, that changes p[i] for subsequent iterations? But within the same iteration, other threads might be reading p[i] while it's being updated if they are computing forces for the